WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.412 --> 00:00:06.910
LEIGHA JARETT: Hi, everybody.

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:09.660
My name is Leigha Jarett, and
I'm a product demonstrations

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.310
manager at Looker.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.280
Today, I'm going to be talking
about how our customers here

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.920
at Google Cloud have
been using data powered

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.250
by BigQuery and Looker to
make data-driven responses

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.145
to COVID-19.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:26.660
I'm going to start by
talking about the impetus

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.920
for creating this data block
that I'll go into in a bit.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.800
I'm going to talk about how
our customers were coming to us

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.340
and needed to analyze
all this publicly

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:40.520
available data for COVID-19.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.460
And then I'm going to jump into
a very quick overview of Looker

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.580
and BigQuery just to give you
context on the different tools

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.140
we're using.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.580
Next, I'm going to walk through
the actual marketplace block

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.620
that Looker team has
launched with the BigQuery

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.600
public datasets team.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.700
And last, I'm going to
talk through a couple

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.790
of our customers' stories,
and how they've actually used

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:04.099
the marketplace block
that we've created

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:06.240
to make data-driven responses.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.750
So, like I said, we began
getting a lot of requests

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:17.830
from our customers asking how
they can use our tools here

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.260
at Google Cloud to
actually analyze

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:24.730
all the data that was becoming
available from COVID-19.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:27.370
As a product demonstrations
manager at Looker,

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:30.970
I'm responsible for creating
different demonstrations of how

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:34.720
we can use Looker to
analyze various data sets.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.620
So many people on our team
were reaching out to me asking,

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.860
how do we create a demo to
show how we can actually

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:45.340
use Looker in BigQuery to
analyze all of this data?

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.110
We were getting requests from
all different industries.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:50.650
We had government
offices contacting us

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.950
who needed data to understand
how they can create

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.790
different policies and state
mitigations to better limit

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:00.110
the spread of the disease.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:01.960
We had different health
care institutions

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.260
that were coming to our
sales teams and our engineers

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.320
asking how they could
use data to prepare

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.290
for an influx of cases
across the world.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:15.010
And, of course, we had all
of our other businesses--

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.520
e-commerce companies,
real estate companies--

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:21.970
all asking us, how
can we really make

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.820
use of all the data
that's become available

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.750
and join it back
with our own data

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:29.500
so we can make
insightful decisions

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.690
and better prepare our
businesses for what's

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:32.680
happening in the world?

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.980
At the start of COVID-19,
different institutions

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:40.660
started releasing data.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:44.020
You could hop on Google and see
tons of different data sources.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.950
Different companies, various
biomedical institutions,

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:50.230
and universities
were all coming out

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.480
with publicly
available data sets

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.760
showing both testing
rates and infection

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:56.540
rates across the world.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.720
It was really overwhelming
amount of data.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:01.630
So our customers
were continuously

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:04.930
coming to us trying to figure
out what data sources to use,

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.150
how to use these data sources.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:09.010
And to make matters
more confusing,

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.230
each data source had
its own definitions

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:13.060
with its own schema.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.480
So it was really difficult to
understand where do we start?

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.122
What data sources do we use?

00:03:18.122 --> 00:03:20.455
And then what do we actually
do with those data sources?

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.380
So just want to
make a quick note

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.170
about the BigQuery
public datasets program.

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:31.300
The BigQuery public
datasets program

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.020
took a large amount of these
different data sources,

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:38.110
created definitions so you
clearly understood what

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:39.970
those data sources,
where they came from,

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:41.770
what the metrics meant.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:45.580
And, of course, they take
care of all of that ETL, the

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:49.930
getting the data into the actual
database for you each day,

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:52.570
so that all of our
BigQuery customers

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:55.750
can simply query this
data and have it available

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:58.100
without needing to figure
out what data sources

00:03:58.100 --> 00:03:59.920
they should use and
how they could get it

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:01.420
into their own databases.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:06.530
The problem with
using this data set

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:10.310
is our analysts at all of
our customers still needed

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:13.280
to figure out what
to do with this data.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.149
And they also had to create the
queries directly in BigQuery

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:20.070
to get answers to
their own questions.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.940
So, of course, a
query is a database

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:25.700
that Google Cloud
powers, but you really

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:30.260
need to understand SQL in order
to be able to ask questions

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:31.710
of this data.

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.190
A lot of our customers
have analysts

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:37.070
who are familiar with
using SQL, but that

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:39.680
means we're limiting who
actually can ask questions

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.890
of the data, and we really
need that technical resource

00:04:42.890 --> 00:04:45.410
to be the person who's
going in and asking

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:46.490
the questions for us.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:50.940
This is where a Looker comes in.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.880
And just to give everyone a
bit of background on Looker

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:57.420
if you don't already know,
Looker is a data platform,

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:00.910
and it's going to fit directly
on top of your SQL database.

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:03.150
So in this case, we're
talking about sitting directly

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:06.910
on top of our public data
sets available in BigQuery.

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:10.800
Now, Looker is powered by a
Git version-controlled modeling

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.550
layer that we call LookML.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:16.200
What LookML allows us
to do is actually create

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.710
a data dictionary of all
of our different metrics.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.950
We use raw SQL to define
those calculations.

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.750
And then business
users, folks who

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.840
are not familiar with
writing SQL queries can

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.620
jump in into the Looker
platform, slice and dice data,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.280
create new reports, export
the data, schedule it.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.710
Basically, they're
empowered to go in and ask

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:40.980
new questions of this data
without needing to rely

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.660
on a technical resource.

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.910
This was also really
important for us,

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:48.580
because like I mentioned
before, the data that

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:52.900
was published with COVID-19 was
not exactly straightforward.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:54.820
We had different companies
that were defining

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:56.680
metrics in different ways.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:58.360
We had data that
was getting updated

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.340
at different frequencies.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:03.460
And putting this all together,
we had a whole team of analysts

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:07.600
here at Looker trying to work
on creating that LookML modeling

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.030
layer to look at the
metrics related to COVID-19.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:13.780
So to give you a little
bit of background,

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:18.250
we had, you know, maybe 10
or 15 of our sales engineers

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:20.770
jumping in, reading
up on that data,

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:24.250
learning the data sources,
understanding the calculations

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:27.280
that were coming out on
places like "New York Times"

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:29.230
or for Johns Hopkins.

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:32.230
And we were creating
our own LookML model

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:34.180
to look at those calculations--

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:36.190
things like number
of new cases that

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:40.000
were emerging each day, daily
growth rates, average growth

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.610
rates across different regions.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:45.460
And we had a pretty
robust process

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:47.980
for going through
our calculations,

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.950
testing things,
making sure everything

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:54.320
was working appropriately.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:57.110
Now, of course, we did all
this to basically create

00:06:57.110 --> 00:07:00.000
these product demonstrations
to show our customers, hey,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.960
this is how you can
actually analyze this data.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:04.800
But after going
through this effort,

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:08.810
we realized we should
make this model that we've

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.960
created available to
all of our customers

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:14.417
so that they don't have to
go through this same process

00:07:14.417 --> 00:07:15.500
that we just went through.

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:22.440
This is where Looker's
Marketplace comes into play.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.380
Looker's Marketplace is
an in-app marketplace.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:29.010
So on your Looker instance,
you can go up, go directly

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:34.400
into the Marketplace, and
install what we call "blocks."

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.930
These blocks are really just
pre-built pieces of LookML,

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:42.460
so that SQL-based modeling
layer, that can help you

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:47.260
be making data decisions,
data-driven decisions faster.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:51.310
What I mean by this is we don't
want our customers spending all

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:54.820
their time trying to understand
what the different data sets

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:58.810
mean, where they're coming from,
how often things are updated,

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:00.520
when they're calculated.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:03.970
We want our customers to
be actually using the data

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:07.450
to make those data-driven
decisions for their business,

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:10.960
because that's what
they're best at doing.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:14.890
So our team, after
creating this LookML model,

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:18.910
we decided to publish it
into the Looker Marketplace.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:20.980
What this means for
our customers is anyone

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:24.830
could go into the
Marketplace, click a button,

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:29.440
download the Marketplace block
into their local instance,

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.049
and have our model that
we've created available

00:08:32.049 --> 00:08:35.440
for them to use, so that
they're really accelerating

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:38.470
their time to value when it
came to these public data sets.

00:08:42.669 --> 00:08:46.060
So when our customers go
and install this block

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:47.560
into their Looker
instance, they're

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:49.460
going to get a few things.

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:51.700
First off, they're going
to get two dashboards that

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.980
are international dashboards.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.470
One, on the left-hand
side, is where

00:08:56.470 --> 00:08:58.840
you can see an
international view of what's

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:01.180
happening across the world.

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:05.290
This data is primarily powered
by Johns Hopkins and "The New

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:07.840
York Times" and some
other data sources

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:13.310
that show things like testing
or government decisions.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:15.920
On the right-hand side,
we can see a dashboard

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:18.140
that's focused on Italy.

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:20.660
Now, this is because the
Italian government published

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:24.410
a few different data sets that
talk in-depth about the case

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:25.780
spread across Italy.

00:09:29.450 --> 00:09:31.880
Next, we have two
other dashboards that

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:34.190
focus on the United States.

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:37.220
First, we have a view of the
entire United States where

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:42.110
we can see things like hospital
bed utilization and infection

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:44.870
rates across the country.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:48.440
From here, we can drill down
into a brand new dashboard

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:51.440
which focuses on a single
state, and it shows you

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:55.010
all the way down to
county-level information.

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:58.970
We're also able to see what the
mitigation and policy response

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:00.950
has been for that state.

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:04.400
And, of course, we can change
filters to only narrow down

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:07.040
to look at a couple of
counties within that state

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:09.020
or compare multiple
states with each other.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:15.450
One other thing I
wanted to mention

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.310
is that more recently,
we've decided

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:20.700
to publish another block
into the Marketplace focused

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:24.300
on the mobility data that's
been published by Google.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:26.790
This data talks about
patterns in mobility

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:28.680
across the United States.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.460
And with both of these blocks
installed on your Looker

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:34.470
instance, you can see
this data side by side

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:37.680
and get a clear view of
how mobility patterns have

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:39.870
been correlated with
different infection

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:41.190
spread across the country.

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.930
Now, when you
download this block

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:51.230
into your Looker instance,
you get those dashboards.

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:55.820
But even better is you have the
Explore environment to actually

00:10:55.820 --> 00:11:00.260
go and slice and dice the data
and ask brand new questions.

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:02.510
As I mentioned before,
any business user

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:04.970
can hop in and ask
these questions,

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:07.880
while Looker is simply
compiling that SQL query

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:11.470
and sending it back to
the query on your behalf.

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:15.510
Now, we notice that this
information can be confusing.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:17.420
And since it's such
sensitive data,

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:19.360
it's really important
for us to make sure

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:22.720
that our customers understand
exactly what these numbers mean

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:25.030
and how they were calculated.

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:27.370
That's why here, you can
see some screenshots I've

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:30.370
taken where you have all
the different tool tips that

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:33.880
show descriptions of how
the metrics are calculated,

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:35.680
descriptions of what
kind of questions

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:39.070
you can ask in this
data-exploration environment,

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:41.180
and links to all
the data sources

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:43.060
so you can go
directly to the source

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:46.340
and understand things like how
they've collected the data.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:52.160
And last, of course, you got
that Looker modeling layer

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:55.130
that's powering this
entire experience.

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:59.420
Like I mentioned, that modeling
layer acts as a data dictionary

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:02.150
where we basically have
this dictionary of all

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:06.380
of our metrics that are powering
both the Explore environment,

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:09.260
and they're powering
those dashboards.

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:11.930
But what's really interesting
about Marketplace blocks

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:16.010
and Looker is that you can
actually extend the block,

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:19.250
and you can change the
actual base configuration, so

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:23.540
modify that data dictionary,
so that it fits your needs.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:25.640
And better yet, you can
extend it and join it

00:12:25.640 --> 00:12:27.870
back on with your own data.

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:30.350
And this is the real
power of this block.

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:34.520
And this is kind of our impetus
for releasing a block like this

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:36.770
is we want you and
all of our customers

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:40.340
to join it back on your data
so you can get increased

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:42.530
insights into what's
really happening

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:46.750
and how it's affecting
your business specifically.

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:48.947
So I hope that after
this presentation,

00:12:48.947 --> 00:12:50.530
you're going to go
ahead and check out

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:52.240
the Looker Marketplace.

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:54.850
We have a lot of
documentation available on how

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:57.400
you can use these blocks
and how you can modify them

00:12:57.400 --> 00:12:59.700
for your own needs.

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:01.590
Additionally, we
wanted to make sure

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:05.370
that you clearly understand how
the modeling layer is working.

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:07.260
So we had our team
of sales engineers

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:12.390
go in and write clear
documentation, robust comments

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:16.050
to really make sure that you're
understanding each calculation

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:16.860
that's performed.

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:23.460
Now what I want to talk about
is a couple of our customers

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:26.400
who are actually using
this block that we built

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:28.860
to help them make decisions
for their business

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:31.550
during this difficult time.

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:34.450
The first I want to
talk about is HCA.

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:38.710
And HCA is one of the nation's
leading health care providers.

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:40.900
At the start of
COVID, they came to us

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:44.530
with a goal of creating a
national response portal.

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:46.390
The goal of this
national response portal

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:49.510
was to give health care
administrators and government

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:52.630
officials a single
place to monitor

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:55.720
the spread of this
disease across the country

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:58.450
so that they can make
better informed decisions

00:13:58.450 --> 00:14:02.290
on both hospital administration
tasks like staffing

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:07.000
and utilization of resources
and also for our government

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.700
officials to be making
data-informed decisions

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:12.210
on policies.

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:15.570
So HCA worked with one
of our partners, SADA,

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:19.470
to build out this portal that
any of the nation's providers

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:23.670
can opt in to and also
contribute data to.

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:26.370
What's really powerful
about what HCA did

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:28.830
is they took the data
that we had worked

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:32.090
with in our block, all this
publicly available data that's

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:33.480
in BigQuery.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:34.950
But they went
ahead and joined it

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:38.580
on their own proprietary
data from these hospitals.

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:42.540
This data centered around things
like ventilator and ICU bed

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:46.350
utilization so that hospitals
can log in at any moment,

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:49.740
focus on one geography,
and see real-time analytics

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:53.040
about what's going on in
that area and, of course,

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:55.680
make better plans for the
future based on the information

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:58.500
that they're seeing.

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:02.370
Now, although COVID-19 was what
inspired this national response

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:06.510
portal to be created, HCA
hopes that this will live on,

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:08.670
because obviously,
this framework is going

00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:12.090
to be really important for
other diseases and just

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:13.320
general public health.

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:20.560
The next customer I'm going to
talk about is Diamond Resorts.

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:23.320
I'm sure many of you have
heard of Diamond Resorts.

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:25.870
They're a timeshare
company with vacation spots

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:28.160
around the whole world.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:30.220
When the pandemic
hit, I'm sure you all

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:33.070
realize that travel
and leisure was

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:37.060
one of the industries that was
hit hardest from the beginning.

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:39.370
People were probably
nervous to travel.

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:41.320
And, of course, there
were restrictions

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:43.540
being placed on where
you could travel,

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:46.690
where you were going to, and
where you could come from.

00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:50.410
So Diamond Resorts had
an influx of requests

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:53.260
coming from their customers
about rescheduling

00:15:53.260 --> 00:15:56.230
their vacations,
canceling their vacations.

00:15:56.230 --> 00:15:58.090
And Diamond Resorts
really needed a way

00:15:58.090 --> 00:16:02.020
to adapt to this
influx of requests.

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:05.370
So what they did was they
used this block as well

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:09.810
and used Looker to analyze
this COVID-19 data, analyze it

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:12.930
alongside their own data
around who is rescheduling,

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:16.980
who's canceling, and tried
to predict when are people

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:18.870
going to want to reschedule?

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:21.660
Where will they be
rescheduling their trips?

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:24.120
And basically, use all
this information to plan

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:28.770
appropriately so that they're
helping their customers get

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:30.780
through this difficult
time and ensure

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:33.690
that they'll be able to
reschedule these vacations when

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:36.540
it's safe to do so and where
and when they want to go.

00:16:40.970 --> 00:16:44.360
The last customer I'm going
to talk about today is Garten.

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:47.420
And Garten delivers
healthy food and snacks

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:50.400
to offices around the country.

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:54.110
So, of course, you can imagine
that when offices shut down

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:57.230
in most of the major cities
in the United States,

00:16:57.230 --> 00:17:00.380
Garten needed a way to
adapt to this change.

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:03.020
Now that things are
beginning to reopen

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:07.119
or we see changes
in our society,

00:17:07.119 --> 00:17:11.000
Garten needed a way to basically
plan for this reopening

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:15.050
and try to understand what
offices would be reopening,

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:17.329
what capacity would
they be reopening at?

00:17:17.329 --> 00:17:20.210
Would they have half of their
employees coming back to work?

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:22.660
Would they only have a handful?

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:24.980
And basically, come
up with programs

00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:28.220
that are going to support
these offices as we're going

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:31.490
through this influx of changes.

00:17:31.490 --> 00:17:36.180
So Garten went ahead and they
used the Looker block as well.

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:38.600
And just like the other
companies I talked about,

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:42.350
they were able to look at this
data alongside their own data

00:17:42.350 --> 00:17:45.890
around what customers they
have, how many employees

00:17:45.890 --> 00:17:49.550
these customers have, where
they are in the United States,

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:51.560
and basically come up
with those programs

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:54.960
to fulfill their
customers' needs.

00:17:54.960 --> 00:17:57.080
So I just want to take
time to say thank you

00:17:57.080 --> 00:18:01.970
all for joining us here
today and listening to how

00:18:01.970 --> 00:18:05.720
our customers are using all
this technology and data to make

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:08.090
these data-driven responses.

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:10.910
I really encourage you
to go understand more

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:14.720
about BigQuery public datasets
program, also, the Looker

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:15.800
Marketplace.

00:18:15.800 --> 00:18:19.040
And you can find really useful
information about this block

00:18:19.040 --> 00:18:23.180
that we've created in this
link that I've posted here.

